  "prediction": "**Apple's Decision to Remove Headphone Jack Sparks Outrage**\n\nAt the recent Apple keynote event, the company announced the removal of the standard 3.5mm aux input, also known as the headphone jack, from its latest iPhone 7 model. Marketing chief Phil Schiller explained the decision by stating that it was a matter of \"courage\" to move on and do something better for all users. However, his words were met with widespread mockery and criticism on social media.\n\nCritics argue that Apple's decision is not about innovation or progress, but rather about profit and control. The removal of the headphone jack forces users to purchase a proprietary Lightning cable or the expensive AirPods, which can cost up to $159. This move has been seen as a way for Apple to increase its revenue and limit user flexibility.\n\nMany experts and users have pointed out that there is no technological excuse for removing the headphone jack. Wired audio is generally considered to be of higher quality than wireless audio, and the use of a proprietary system like Lightning or AirPods can lead to connectivity issues and poor sound quality.\n\nThe decision has also been criticized for its impact on users who rely on the headphone jack for their daily activities, such as listening to music or taking phone calls. The removal of the headphone jack has been seen as a step backward, rather than a step forward, and has sparked a large amount of outrage and frustration among users.\n\nIn response to the criticism, Apple has touted the benefits of its AirPods, which offer wireless connectivity and a sleek design. However, many users have expressed concerns about the cost and practicality of the AirPods, as well as the potential for them to be lost or damaged.\n\nOverall, Apple's decision to remove the headphone jack from its iPhone 7 model has been met with widespread criticism and outrage. The move has been seen as a way for the company to increase its revenue and control user behavior, rather than a genuine attempt to innovate or improve the user experience.",
